00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:24.730
OK, and so we don't persist
that secret, which is

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:26.270
necessary to unlock the data.

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:29.850
And so for that reason, if
you don't access the data,

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:33.310
it's really, really, really hard
for us to actually get access

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:34.030
to anything.

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:36.610
Now, the one thing that I just
want to point out about that

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:39.280
is that it's only
available for GCS and GCE,

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:43.570
and a good reason for this
is that we really don't have

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:45.610
access to that key otherwise.

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:48.550
And so if we don't
have access to the key,

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:50.110
we can't do things
that are offline,

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:53.470
like compaction or logical
backups or anything like that.

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:56.930
So it'll only be for
those two services.

00:02:56.930 --> 00:02:59.270
Now, what's an HSM?

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:02.810
How many people here use an HSM?

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:06.410
OK, good-- actually, I
would say over half of you.

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:10.480
So for the other half, an HSM
is a hardware security module.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:12.370
It's basically a
physical device that's

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:16.110
used for managing and
performing crypt operations.

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:20.598
Now, there's a lot of
reasons why people use these.

00:03:20.598 --> 00:03:22.390
You can create keys
that are non exportable

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:24.760
and the hardware device will
provide certain guarantees

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:28.930
to prevent the keys from
being exported or exposed.

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:31.480
And they often provide
some guarantees around

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:33.550
tamper evident and
tamper resistance.

00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:36.680
So things like if you
try to extract the key,

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:38.680
it'll be something that
you'll be made aware of.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:41.440
And in the extreme cases,
if they can't potentially

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:44.110
zero out the keys, as well,
so that the attacker can't

00:03:44.110 --> 00:03:46.480
actually get access to anything.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:49.120
And the final thing there
is that they are often

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:52.140
FIPS 140-2 certified.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:56.330
FIPS 140-2 is a
certification from NIST

00:03:56.330 --> 00:03:58.225
that basically defines
crypto modules,

00:03:58.225 --> 00:04:00.350
and so it covers everything
from the implementation

00:04:00.350 --> 00:04:04.400
of algorithms to the way the
random numbers are generated

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:05.930
and everything else.

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:07.910
Most times people are
using these devices

00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:10.820
because they either are
very heavily regulated

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:13.760
and so these things are
often helpful in getting

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:16.279
through your compliance
auditor, or they're

00:04:16.279 --> 00:04:18.560
required to protect
high valued keys,

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.399
so financial
institutions, for example,

00:04:20.399 --> 00:04:22.590
are very, very big
on these things.

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:24.267
Now, what we've
done with Cloud HSM

00:04:24.267 --> 00:04:25.850
is that we provide
the managed service

00:04:25.850 --> 00:04:27.630
on top of these devices.

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:30.007
So for the other half who
have actually had HSMs,

00:04:30.007 --> 00:04:32.090
you know that they can be
a little bit challenging

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:34.070
sometimes to actually manage.

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:37.670
And so we have taken that
burden and we manage everything

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:40.250
to the point where
all you see is an API

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:42.740
to do the key management
and do key generation

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:45.260
encryption/decryption,
things like that.

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:47.670
And so there's really
nothing for you to do.

00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:51.740
We actually manage it
and we fully scale it.

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:53.450
We do auto patching.

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:56.520
We do the backups of
the keys, everything.

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:58.400
So there's nothing
for you to do.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:04.420
Now, one of the things
that you might be asking

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:07.360
is why would I
use our Cloud HSM?

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:10.630
Well, compliance is, again,
a major motivator for this.

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:15.580
We are using FIPS 140-2
Level 3 certified.

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:17.613
And so that may or
may not be important.

00:05:17.613 --> 00:05:19.030
If you don't know
what that means,

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:21.920
then probably this
doesn't matter to you.

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:23.640
But if you do
care, then you know

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:25.750
that Level 3 is sort
of the gold standard

00:05:25.750 --> 00:05:28.220
that everyone looks for now.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:31.000
And you can only achieve Level
3 through hardware devices,

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:33.060
you can't do this with software.

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:36.100
Regionalization-- so one of
the interesting properties

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:38.080
of the service
that we've created

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:42.790
is that when you create a
key in our Cloud HSM service,

00:05:42.790 --> 00:05:45.010
that key is
cryptographically bound

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:47.120
to the HSMs within that region.

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:49.930
So if you create a key
in, say, US Central 1,

00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:53.710
then only those
HSMs in US Central 1

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:56.020
are able to actually
go and use those keys

00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:57.860
and perform crypto operations.

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:00.550
OK, it's just that
there's a strong binding

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:01.990
cryptographically
between the keys

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:04.530
and the devices themselves.

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:06.370
We will eventually
support a global region

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:08.920
and we're working on
multi-regions right now.

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:11.740
But currently we have this
deployed in several regions

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:14.470
across the United
States and Europe.

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:17.440
And for those people who
are already using Cloud KMS,

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:20.380
then there's actually
no changes necessary.

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:21.330
It's the same API.

00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:24.520
It's just one flag when you
actually create the key,

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:27.340
and then everything else
is exactly the same.

