00:07:32.390 --> 00:07:33.547
HSM.

00:07:33.547 --> 00:07:35.630
OK, and it'd be done in a
very secure manner where

00:07:35.630 --> 00:07:37.963
you will not have to worry
about the keys being exposed.

00:07:37.963 --> 00:07:40.400
Now, in the beta,
we'll expand this

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:42.910
to support other types
of keys and import it

00:07:42.910 --> 00:07:44.010
into the software.

00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:46.800
But for now, you can actually
import this into the HSMs.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:51.500
And the way that works is
that we use a method called--

00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:54.680
well, it's a [INAUDIBLE]
method, so just forgive me

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:56.630
for a brief moment
where I step in

00:07:56.630 --> 00:07:58.980
to a little but more
technical jargon.

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:03.230
But we're using the RSA AES Key
Wrap, and essentially the way

00:08:03.230 --> 00:08:06.440
that the process works is
that the HSMs will create

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:10.490
a public private key pair with
the private key only available

00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:13.620
within the HSM, so we
don't have access to it.

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:17.300
And then you can
export that public key,

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:19.545
take it to your key
management device,

00:08:19.545 --> 00:08:21.170
and then on your key
management device,

00:08:21.170 --> 00:08:23.770
you would have to do this
process called AES Key Wrap.

00:08:23.770 --> 00:08:25.160
And essentially,
the process says

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:27.860
is that your key manager
would have to go and create

00:08:27.860 --> 00:08:30.530
an AES Key to wrap
your key, or the key

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:31.700
that you want to import.

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:33.770
And then the public key
that you just download

00:08:33.770 --> 00:08:36.740
would wrap the AES Key, and then
you'd take that and bring that

00:08:36.740 --> 00:08:38.090
back up to our cloud.

00:08:38.090 --> 00:08:41.210
And then our HSMs are
able to unwrap everything,

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:42.868
all within the HSM boundary.

00:08:42.868 --> 00:08:44.660
MATTHEW DRISCOLL: My
name is Matt Driscoll.

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:46.618
As I said at the beginning,
I'm product manager

00:08:46.618 --> 00:08:48.930
and I focus a lot on Customer
Managed Encryption Keys.

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:50.310
So what is CMEK?

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:53.390
CMEK is a feature that allows
you to control the encryption

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:55.770
keys that are used to
protect your data at rest

00:08:55.770 --> 00:08:56.830
in Google Cloud.

00:08:56.830 --> 00:08:58.580
This is a really,
really important feature

00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:01.070
if you need to show control
for regulatory or compliance

00:09:01.070 --> 00:09:03.890
reasons over the
protection of your data.

00:09:03.890 --> 00:09:05.840
We'll talk in a second
in a little more

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:08.150
detail about where this
is available on GCP today.

00:09:08.150 --> 00:09:11.420
But just very quickly, supported
in BigQuery, Compute Engine,

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:13.280
Persistent Disk,
in cloud storage,

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:15.840
and in our Dataproc product.

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:17.835
So how does CMEK work?

00:09:17.835 --> 00:09:19.460
Actually, to understand
how CMEK works,

00:09:19.460 --> 00:09:21.793
you first need to know a
little bit about how encryption

00:09:21.793 --> 00:09:23.040
at rest works within Google.

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:24.582
So Il-Sung mentioned
at the beginning

00:09:24.582 --> 00:09:26.030
that we provide
default encryption

00:09:26.030 --> 00:09:29.930
at rest for all of your
data in Google Cloud.

00:09:29.930 --> 00:09:30.920
So we'll start there.

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:33.410
When we provide this default
encryption at rest, what we do

00:09:33.410 --> 00:09:36.140
is we take data as it
comes in and we split it

00:09:36.140 --> 00:09:37.740
into small chunks.

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:39.380
Each of those chunks
of data is then

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:41.300
encrypted with a
data encryption key.

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:45.080
Then that data and it's
encrypted data encryption key

00:09:45.080 --> 00:09:47.390
is distributed across Google's
storage infrastructure

00:09:47.390 --> 00:09:48.180
and stored.

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:50.630
So when you come
to Google and you

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:54.440
decide that default encryption
at rest is sufficient,

00:09:54.440 --> 00:09:57.380
this is generally the
scheme that we use.

00:09:57.380 --> 00:09:59.780
So when you use CMEK,
we're adding essentially

00:09:59.780 --> 00:10:01.280
an extra layer into this.

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:03.810
Each chunk is still encrypted
with a data encryption key.

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:07.370
But then that data encryption
key is wrapped with a KMS key.

00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:10.130
We call that KMS key
the key encryption key.

00:10:10.130 --> 00:10:12.710
So what will happen when
you put the data to rest

00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:15.530
is the service that is storing
your data will call out to KMS,

00:10:15.530 --> 00:10:18.260
it will wrap the data
encryption key with the KMS key

00:10:18.260 --> 00:10:19.983
that you've
indicated, and then it

00:10:19.983 --> 00:10:21.650
will store the encrypted
data encryption

00:10:21.650 --> 00:10:24.360
key and the data chunk in
whatever storage system

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:24.927
it's using.

00:10:24.927 --> 00:10:26.510
So there's some
interesting properties

00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:28.760
of this that are really,
really useful for compliance.

00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:30.470
The most important
is auditability.

00:10:30.470 --> 00:10:32.330
So every time that
we put data to rest,

00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:35.000
we actually call out to KMS,
and so you can get a data access

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:38.900
log and a log of the encrypt or
decrypt of the data encryption

00:10:38.900 --> 00:10:39.410
key.

00:10:39.410 --> 00:10:42.890
And you can use that to go
monitor access to your data

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:45.590
through our key
management system.

00:10:45.590 --> 00:10:49.250
Also to the services
that use KMS for CMEK,

00:10:49.250 --> 00:10:51.702
it's transparent if
the key is in an HSM

00:10:51.702 --> 00:10:52.910
or if the key is in software.

00:10:52.910 --> 00:10:55.923
So all of the sort of value
that you get from cloud HSM

00:10:55.923 --> 00:10:57.590
is available with the
same configuration

00:10:57.590 --> 00:10:59.230
that you would use
for a software key.

00:10:59.230 --> 00:11:00.230
This is really powerful.

00:11:00.230 --> 00:11:03.050
Like, we actually
totally manage, make it

00:11:03.050 --> 00:11:04.950
totally transparent
to you, the customer,

00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:08.030
that we have HSMs behind the
scenes protecting your data.

00:11:08.030 --> 00:11:10.130
So what are some best
practices for CMEK?

00:11:10.130 --> 00:11:11.760
There's two things
I want to highlight.

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:13.665
First is regional alignment.

00:11:13.665 --> 00:11:15.290
When you actually go
to configure CMEK,

00:11:15.290 --> 00:11:17.420
you'll notice that
some of the services

00:11:17.420 --> 00:11:18.962
will only allow you
to configure keys

00:11:18.962 --> 00:11:21.087
that are in the same region
as the resource they're

00:11:21.087 --> 00:11:21.830
protecting.

00:11:21.830 --> 00:11:26.090
We push customers to do this
for resiliency and for latency.

00:11:26.090 --> 00:11:27.950
And it's occasionally
a place where

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:30.770
folks stub their toe if they
don't know this constraint.

00:11:30.770 --> 00:11:32.420
And the second
separation of duties.

00:11:32.420 --> 00:11:36.770
So how the services that
store your data talk to KMS

00:11:36.770 --> 00:11:39.220
is through a service account.

00:11:39.220 --> 00:11:41.470
When we provision that service
account on your behalf,

00:11:41.470 --> 00:11:43.170
or we have tools
to do this for you

00:11:43.170 --> 00:11:46.402
when you create CMEK
protected resources in the UI,

00:11:46.402 --> 00:11:48.360
we give that service
account a very minimal set

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:48.985
of permissions.

00:11:48.985 --> 00:11:51.780
It only can encrypt and
decrypt with the KMS key.

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:53.280
So when you're using
KMS in general,

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:54.930
we encourage you to
think very deeply

00:11:54.930 --> 00:11:57.270
about what permissions are
actually needed for any given

00:11:57.270 --> 00:12:01.310
principle accessing KMS and keep
those permissions to a minimum.

00:12:01.310 --> 00:12:03.710
So CMEK support on Google Cloud.

00:12:03.710 --> 00:12:05.645
Today, we support CMEK
across four services.

00:12:05.645 --> 00:12:07.760
And I want to give you
some detail about it.

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:09.900
In BigQuery, we support
CMEK at the table level.

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:12.900
So anytime you create a table,
you can indicate a CMEK key,

00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:16.310
and we will use that CMEK key to
protect the table in BigQuery.

00:12:16.310 --> 00:12:19.255
In Dataproc, we support
CMEK at the cluster level.

00:12:19.255 --> 00:12:20.630
That CMEK key is
going to be used

00:12:20.630 --> 00:12:23.310
to protect the persistent
disks that are in the cluster.

00:12:23.310 --> 00:12:26.960
We also allow you to
indicate a storage bucket.

00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:31.230
And that storage bucket can be
protected using CMEK as well.

00:12:31.230 --> 00:12:35.060
And so implicitly, cloud
storage also supports CMEK.

00:12:35.060 --> 00:12:38.330
And we support CMEK as a
default key at the bucket level

00:12:38.330 --> 00:12:41.730
or as an object-level CMEK
key when you write an object.

00:12:41.730 --> 00:12:45.470
And finally, Persistent
Disk supports CMEK per disk.

